  "transcript": "A pre-existing condition is one for which the insured received medical advice, diagnosis or treatment, or had symptoms a prudent person would have treated, during a look-back period before coverage began, often six to twelve months. Older medical policies excluded such conditions for a waiting period. Federal law now forbids pre-existing condition exclusions in comprehensive medical coverage, so Marcus’s asthma is covered from day one. The exclusion still appears in disability income, long-term care, Medicare supplement, short-term policies and certain other limited policies. Each type has its own rules. Always check the type of coverage before applying the rule.\n\nPolicies exclude intentionally self-inflicted injuries, including injuries from a suicide attempt, because insurance covers accidental and unexpected loss. Mental health treatment for the underlying condition is a separate, covered matter under parity rules. Policies also exclude losses caused by war or an act of war, declared or undeclared, and often losses while serving in the armed forces, for which military coverage exists. If Rosa deliberately injures herself, her disability policy does not pay; if she is injured in a terrorist attack at home, most policies pay because that is not war between nations.\n\nElective cosmetic surgery, chosen to change appearance without a medical need, is excluded. Reconstructive surgery is different. Repairing a face after an accident, correcting a congenital defect in a child, or rebuilding a breast after a mastectomy restores function or appearance lost to injury or disease, and these are covered. Maryland specifically requires coverage of reconstructive breast surgery after a mastectomy, including surgery on the other breast for symmetry. The question to ask is why the procedure is being done. A medical cause moves the claim out of the exclusion.\n\nMost individual and group health policies are nonoccupational. They exclude conditions covered by workers compensation, because that system is the primary payer for job-related injuries and illnesses. When Rosa is hurt on a job site, workers compensation pays her medical bills and partial wage replacement, and her health plan steps aside. Policies also exclude care furnished by government plans, such as treatment at a Veterans Affairs facility or under military coverage, and care for which the insured has no legal obligation to pay. The purpose is to avoid paying twice for the same loss.\n\nInjuries sustained while committing or attempting a felony, or while engaged in an illegal occupation, are excluded. The uniform optional provision on illegal occupation, which Lesson 17 covers, lets the insurer deny a loss arising from such activity. A person injured while burglarizing a warehouse cannot collect medical or disability benefits for those injuries. The exclusion requires participation in the crime; a bystander or victim injured during someone else’s felony is covered. Some policies similarly exclude losses while intoxicated or under the influence of unprescribed narcotics, again through an optional provision.\n\nExclusions are lists of causes, not lists of body parts. To decide a claim, identify the cause of the loss, then check it against the list. Asthma under a comprehensive medical plan: covered, because pre-existing exclusions are gone. A deliberate overdose: excluded as self-inflicted. A scar revision after a dog bite: covered as reconstructive. A fall at work: workers compensation, not the health plan. A broken leg during a robbery the insured committed: excluded. When two exclusions seem to apply, the one that actually caused the loss controls, and the burden of proving an exclusion generally falls on the insurer."
